        fullContent: 'Neville draws on both mystical tradition and scientific experimentation to make a profound point: the world you perceive is not an objective reality existing "out there" — it is an assumptive world. What you see when you look at something depends not so much on what is there as on the assumptions you bring to the act of perception. Your assumptions literally shape what you perceive, and what you perceive reinforces your assumptions in a self-creating loop.\n\nThis is not merely about selective attention or cognitive bias (though those are crude approximations of the principle). Neville is saying something far more radical: the physical world itself is an assumption crystallized into form. The seemingly solid, independent reality you observe is actually consciousness frozen into the pattern of your assumptions. Change the assumption, and the world must change to reflect it.\n\nNeville cites experiments in which observers with different assumptions perceive entirely different phenomena when looking at the same object. This demonstrates that perception is not passive reception of data but active creation through assumption. The implications are staggering — it means that your world is not happening TO you but THROUGH you.\n\nWhen you truly grasp that you live in an assumptive world, you realize that no condition has inherent reality independent of your assumption about it. Poverty, illness, loneliness — these are not fixed conditions but assumptions that have hardened into fact. Change the assumption, and the condition must yield. The world is plastic to your assumption — it has no choice in the matter.',
