        fullContent: 'This single sentence — "An assumption, though false, if persisted in, will harden into fact" — is the cornerstone of Neville\'s entire teaching. It is the law stated in its simplest form. The word "persisted" is the crucial qualifier. An assumption that is merely entertained for a moment will not manifest. It must be sustained, maintained, and dwelt upon until it becomes the dominant state of consciousness.\n\nNeville interprets the entire story of Jesus as an allegory of persistent assumption. The crucifixion represents the fixing (crucifying) of the new concept of self upon the cross of the old nature. The resurrection represents the manifestation of the assumption in the external world. "The story of Jesus is persistent assumption" — not a historical account of a man who lived 2000 years ago, but a psychological drama that takes place within every individual who dares to assume a new state of being.\n\nThe process of hardening involves the subconscious. When you sustain an assumption through feeling, you are literally rewiring the subconscious mind. The subconscious, which controls all bodily functions, habits, and automatic responses, gradually aligns every aspect of your being with the new assumption. Your thoughts, words, actions, and even your physiology begin to reflect the assumed state. This inner alignment inevitably produces corresponding changes in the external world.\n\nThe time lag between assumption and manifestation is not a failure of the law but a necessary interval during which the assumption "hardens." During this interval, your faith is tested. The 3D world will continue to show evidence of the old assumption. But if you persist — if you refuse to accept the evidence of your senses — the new assumption will eventually crystallize into physical fact. It cannot fail.',
