        title: 'Importunity: Kelancangan yang Berani',
        bullets: ['Karena importunity pria ini, ia turun', 'Tolak jawaban tidak dari pikiran Anda sendiri', 'Persistensi yang berani mengalahkan semua resistensi'],
        fullContent: 'Importunity is one of Neville\'s most distinctive teachings, drawn from the parable in Luke 11:5-8 where a man obtains bread from his friend at midnight "because of his importunity" — his shameless, brazen persistence. Neville interprets this parable psychologically: the friend at midnight is your own subconscious mind, and importunity is the attitude you must adopt when impressing it with your desire.\n\nImportunity means refusing to take "no" for an answer — not from the external world, but from your own mind. It is the brazen impudence to assume you already have what reason and senses deny. It is the audacity to claim a state as present fact when every external indicator says otherwise. This is not arrogance toward the universe — it is the recognition that the universe operates by law, and the law requires you to assume the feeling of the wish fulfilled.\n\nMost people fail not because the law doesn\'t work but because they give up too soon. They assume the feeling for a day or two, see no change, and conclude the law has failed. But the law has not failed — they have failed to persist. Importunity means you keep coming back to the assumption no matter how many times doubt or appearances try to dissuade you. You are importunate with your own subconscious, refusing to let it settle back into the old pattern.\n\nThe practical application is to adopt an attitude of confident insistence — not anxious desperation, but calm, assured demand. You know the law works. You know the assumption must harden. Therefore you persist with the quiet confidence of one who has already received. "He would not come down, but because of this man\'s importunity he came down and gave him all that he needed."',
